"My hypothesis is that a DDoS attack on an IoT device differs from normal traffic due to small packet sizes and low transmission intensity. This is counterintuitive, as classic DDoS attacks are associated with 'noise' and high speeds. However, the reality of IoT traffic is the opposite: normal traffic consists of large firmware updates and data streams, whereas a DDoS attack involves a multitude of small, frequent requests—insignificant in terms of volume but capable of overloading the device through sheer quantity. After testing this hypothesis in Splunk using 10,000 records, I achieved a precision of 94.33%—meaning that 94 out of every 100 detected events were indeed DDoS attacks."
